{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "def is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is undulating, meaning its digits alternate between increasing and decreasing.\n    Single-digit numbers are considered undulating.\n    \"\"\"\n    s_number = str(abs(number))\n    n = len(s_number)\n\n    if n <= 2:\n        return True\n\n    # Pattern: digit[i] < digit[i+1] > digit[i+2] or digit[i] > digit[i+1] < digit[i+2]\n    # Check the first pair to establish the initial trend\n    # If s_number[0] == s_number[1], it cannot be undulating\n    if s_number[0] == s_number[1]:\n        return False\n\n    initial_increasing = (s_number[0] < s_number[1])\n\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        d1 = int(s_number[i])\n        d2 = int(s_number[i+1])\n        d3 = int(s_number[i+2])\n\n        if d1 == d2 or d2 == d3: # Adjacent digits must be different\n            return False\n\n        if initial_increasing: # Expecting < > < > ...\n            # Current pattern should be d1 < d2 > d3\n            if not (d1 < d2 and d2 > d3):\n                return False\n            # For the next comparison, the pattern flips (d2 > d3 < d4)\n            # So, we toggle initial_increasing if we are looking at groups of 3\n            # But here we are checking the _overall_ alternating pattern\n            # For an undulating number, every 'peak' must be preceded by a 'valley' and vice versa.\n            # d1 < d2 (first part of current expected pattern)\n            # d2 > d3 (second part of current expected pattern)\n            # If the initial trend was d1 < d2, then for the next pair (d2,d3), the trend should be d2 > d3.\n            # And for the pair (d3,d4), the trend should be d3 < d4.\n            # This logic needs to check if the current 'turn' is consistent with the initial turn.\n            # Let's re-evaluate:\n            # d_i < d_{i+1} and d_{i+1} > d_{i+2}  OR\n            # d_i > d_{i+1} and d_{i+1} < d_{i+2}\n\n            # If the first two digits were d0 < d1, then we expect the sequence to be < > < > ...\n            # So for any i, d_i < d_{i+1} must be true if i is even, and d_i > d_{i+1} if i is odd.\n            # OR vice versa if d0 > d1.\n            \n            # Simplified approach:\n            # Check d_i vs d_{i+1}\n            # The trend (d_i < d_{i+1} or d_i > d_{i+1}) must alternate\n            \n            # Initial trend:\n            # If initial_increasing is True, we expect: d0 < d1, d1 > d2, d2 < d3, d3 > d4 ...\n            # If initial_increasing is False, we expect: d0 > d1, d1 < d2, d2 > d3, d3 < d4 ...\n            \n            # Check for current position i:\n            # The relationship between s_number[i] and s_number[i+1] must be\n            # (initial_increasing XOR (i % 2 != 0)) -> This defines the expected < or > for the current pair\n            \n            # i = 0: (initial_increasing XOR False) -> initial_increasing\n            # If initial_increasing is True, expect s_number[0] < s_number[1]\n            # If initial_increasing is False, expect s_number[0] > s_number[1]\n            \n            # i = 1: (initial_increasing XOR True) -> NOT initial_increasing\n            # If initial_increasing is True, expect s_number[1] > s_number[2]\n            # If initial_increasing is False, expect s_number[1] < s_number[2]\n\n            expected_increasing = initial_increasing if (i % 2 == 0) else not initial_increasing\n\n            current_increasing = (d1 < d2)\n            if current_increasing != expected_increasing:\n                return False\n            \n            # Now, check the next segment (d2, d3) in relation to the initial trend\n            # The relationship between s_number[i+1] and s_number[i+2] must be\n            # (initial_increasing XOR ((i+1) % 2 != 0))\n            expected_increasing_next = initial_increasing if ((i+1) % 2 == 0) else not initial_increasing\n            current_increasing_next = (d2 < d3)\n            if current_increasing_next != expected_increasing_next:\n                return False\n                \n    return True"}
