{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the number of possible sequences of length n, such that each element is a positive integer\n    and is greater than or equal to twice the previous element but less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    memo = {}\n\n    def _count_recursive(current_len: int, last_element: int) -> int:\n        # Base case: if current_len equals n, a complete sequence of length n has been formed.\n        if current_len == n:\n            return 1\n\n        # Check memoization table to avoid redundant calculations.\n        if (current_len, last_element) in memo:\n            return memo[(current_len, last_element)]\n\n        total_ways = 0\n        # The next element must be at least twice the last element.\n        min_next_element = 2 * last_element\n\n        # Iterate through possible values for the next element, ensuring it does not exceed m.\n        for next_element in range(min_next_element, m + 1):\n            # Recursively count sequences for the remaining length.\n            total_ways += _count_recursive(current_len + 1, next_element)\n\n        # Store the computed result in the memoization table before returning.\n        memo[(current_len, last_element)] = total_ways\n        return total_ways\n\n    total_sequences = 0\n    # The first element of the sequence can be any positive integer from 1 to m.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_sequences += _count_recursive(1, first_element)\n\n    return total_sequences"}
