{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(input: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if input < 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Find the integer square root of the input\n    root = math.isqrt(input)\n\n    # If root*root is already greater than input, it's the next perfect square.\n    # Otherwise, the next perfect square is (root+1)^2.\n    if root * root > input:\n        return root * root\n    else:\n        return (root + 1) ** 2"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Per BR1, array must contain at least one integer.\n    # If the array is empty, which shouldn't happen per BR1, we could return 0 or raise an error.\n    # For a non-empty array, the subsequent loops will initialize max_bitonic_sum.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # lis_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i].\n    lis_sum = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis_sum[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[j] < arr[i]:\n                lis_sum[i] = max(lis_sum[i], lis_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # lds_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i].\n    lds_sum = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds_sum[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[j] < arr[i]:\n                lds_sum[i] = max(lds_sum[i], lds_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # The maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence is the maximum of (lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i])\n    # for all i. We subtract arr[i] once because it's counted in both lis_sum[i] and lds_sum[i].\n    max_bitonic_sum = float('-inf')\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming with O(n^2) time and space complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of s[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromic subsequences of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths (gaps) from 2 to n\n    # 'cl' is current length of the substring\n    for cl in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - cl + 1):\n            j = i + cl - 1  # j is the end index of the current substring\n\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters match, add 2 to the LPS of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                # If cl=2, then i+1 > j-1, so dp[i+1][j-1] would be 0 from initialization, resulting in 2.\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1] + 2\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of\n                # LPS of s[i+1...j] (excluding s[i]) and LPS of s[i...j-1] (excluding s[j]).\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n\n    return dp[0][n-1]"}
