{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth Newman\u2013Shanks\u2013Williams prime number.\n    The function interprets `n` such that `newman_prime(3)` returns `7`.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(num):\n        # Checks if a number is prime using trial division.\n        # This is efficient enough for the required range of NSW numbers.\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        if num == 2:\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 3\n        while i * i <= num:\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n\n    # Initialize the Newman\u2013Shanks\u2013Williams sequence: S_0 = 1, S_1 = 1, S_k = 2*S_{k-1} + S_{k-2}\n    s_prev_prev = 1\n    s_prev = 1\n\n    found_primes = []\n\n    # Generate NSW numbers and collect primes until `n` primes are found.\n    # The first NSW number to check for primality is S_2, which is 3.\n    while len(found_primes) < n:\n        s_current = 2 * s_prev + s_prev_prev\n        \n        if is_prime(s_current):\n            found_primes.append(s_current)\n        \n        s_prev_prev = s_prev\n        s_prev = s_current\n    \n    # Based on the example `newman_prime(3) == 7`, `n` corresponds to the (n-1)-th prime.\n    # Since `found_primes` is 0-indexed, this means we return `found_primes[n-2]`.\n    return found_primes[n - 2]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/261", "solution": "def division_elements(t1, t2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs element-wise division of two tuples, returning a new tuple.\n    Raises ZeroDivisionError if any element in the second tuple is zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(t1) != len(t2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuples must have the same length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(t1)):\n        if t2[i] == 0:\n            raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot divide by zero.\")\n        result.append(t1[i] / t2[i])\n    return tuple(result)"}
