{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr, n, i, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from prefix 0..i that includes the element at index k.\n    Assumes k > i.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not (0 <= i < n and 0 <= k < n and k > i):\n        # Based on the design document, we assume inputs are valid or handled by external validation.\n        # This check is conceptually part of validation, but given \"no raise exceptions\" constraint,\n        # we'll proceed assuming valid input structure and indices.\n        pass\n\n    dp = [0] * (i + 1)\n    \n    # Initialize dp[j] to be the value of arr[j] if it's considered as a subsequence of length 1\n    # that can potentially be part of an increasing subsequence ending at j.\n    # However, since we MUST include arr[k], we need to ensure that condition is met.\n    \n    max_overall_sum = 0\n    \n    # Check if arr[k] can start an increasing subsequence\n    # The requirement is that the subsequence is from prefix 0..i AND includes arr[k].\n    # This implies that arr[k] must be greater than some arr[j] where j <= i,\n    # or arr[k] itself is part of the subsequence.\n    # The wording \"from prefix until ith index and also including a given kth element\"\n    # implies that the increasing subsequence itself ends at some j <= i, AND arr[k] is included\n    # AND arr[k] is greater than arr[j]. This is a bit ambiguous.\n    # Let's re-interpret: \"max sum of increasing subsequence {s_0, s_1, ..., s_m}\n    # where all s_x are from arr[0..i], and arr[k] is part of this sequence,\n    # and k > i, meaning arr[k] is just 'a given element' that must be included,\n    # and it is greater than the last element of the prefix subsequence.\"\n\n    # Let's follow the assertion: max_sum_increasing_subseq([1, 101, 2, 3, 100, 4, 5 ], 7, 4, 6) == 11\n    # Here, arr = [1, 101, 2, 3, 100, 4, 5], n=7, i=4, k=6\n    # Prefix 0..i is [1, 101, 2, 3, 100]\n    # arr[k] = arr[6] = 5\n    # The expected result is 11.\n    # This corresponds to subsequence [1, 2, 3] from the prefix, and then including arr[k]=5.\n    # The sum is 1+2+3+5 = 11.\n    # This interpretation means the increasing subsequence is formed by elements from arr[0...i]\n    # AND arr[k], where arr[k] must be greater than the largest element from arr[0...i] that is part of the subsequence.\n\n    # dp[j] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index j (j <= i)\n    # that *could potentially* be extended by arr[k].\n    \n    # Initialize dp values for the prefix 0..i\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        dp[j] = arr[j]\n\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        for prev in range(j):\n            if arr[j] > arr[prev]:\n                dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[prev] + arr[j])\n\n    # Now, dp[j] contains the max sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[j] for j <= i.\n    # We need to find the max sum from these subsequences, such that arr[k] can extend it\n    # AND arr[k] is included.\n    \n    # The \"including a given kth element which is after i\" part needs careful interpretation.\n    # Based on the example, arr[k] MUST be greater than the last element of the prefix subsequence.\n    # So, we iterate through all possible ending points 'j' in the prefix (0 to i).\n    # If arr[k] is greater than arr[j], then arr[k] can extend the increasing subsequence ending at j.\n    # We add arr[k] to dp[j] to get a candidate sum.\n    \n    max_total_sum = 0\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        if arr[k] > arr[j]:\n            max_total_sum = max(max_total_sum, dp[j] + arr[k])\n\n    # There's an edge case where no element in arr[0...i] is smaller than arr[k],\n    # or arr[k] is the smallest element overall.\n    # The requirement implicitly states that the subsequence starts from prefix and includes k.\n    # If arr[k] cannot extend any existing increasing subsequence ending in 0..i,\n    # it might still form a subsequence like [arr[x], ..., arr[j], arr[k]] where arr[k] is the only element\n    # if no such arr[j] exists. This implies the problem could also mean \"find the max IS that is\n    # composed of elements from [0..i] U {k}, and k is always included\".\n    # The problem \"max sum of increasing subsequence from prefix until ith index and also including a given kth element\"\n    # combined with the example (1,2,3 from prefix + 5 from arr[k]) clearly implies arr[k] is appended.\n\n    # If no such 'j' exists where arr[k] > arr[j], it means arr[k] cannot extend any subsequence.\n    # In this case, max_total_sum would be 0.\n    # However, the problem statement implies arr[k] *must* be included.\n    # If arr[k] is the smallest value (e.g. arr[k]=1, but prefix elements are 2,3,4), it cannot\n    # extend any of them. But an IS must include it.\n    # This could mean the result is just arr[k] if no elements from prefix can come before it.\n    # But the assertion `max_sum_increasing_subseq([1, 101, 2, 3, 100, 4, 5 ], 7, 4, 6) == 11` (1+2+3+5)\n    # implies we are building upon a prefix subsequence.\n    \n    # If arr[k] is not greater than any arr[j] (j <= i), it means arr[k] cannot extend any\n    # *existing* increasing subsequence from the prefix. In this specific scenario,\n    # the maximum sum could potentially be just arr[k] itself if that's the only increasing subsequence\n    # that *includes* arr[k] (e.g., [arr[k]]).\n    # However, the phrasing \"from prefix until ith index and also including a given kth element\" strongly\n    # suggests a structure like `(IS from prefix) + arr[k]`.\n    # Let's stick to the interpretation aligned with the example: arr[k] extends a prefix IS.\n    # If no such IS can be extended, the sum is 0.\n\n    return max_total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/310", "solution": "class StringConverter:\n    \"\"\"A helper class to convert strings to tuples of characters.\"\"\"\n    def convert(self, input_string: str) -> tuple:\n        \"\"\"Converts the given string into a tuple of its characters.\"\"\"\n        return tuple(input_string)\n\ndef string_to_tuple(input_string: str) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Converts a given string to a tuple of characters.\"\"\"\n    converter = StringConverter()\n    return converter.convert(input_string)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "from typing import List\n\ndef large_product(list1: List[int], list2: List[int], k: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the k largest products from all pairwise combinations of list1 and list2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not list1 or not list2 or k <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    products = [a * b for a in list1 for b in list2]\n    products.sort(reverse=True)\n    \n    return products[:k]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/312", "solution": "import math\n\ndef volume_cone(radius: float, height: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the volume of a right circular cone given its radius and height.\"\"\"\n    if radius <= 0 or height <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Radius and height must be positive values.\")\n    \n    return (1/3) * math.pi * (radius**2) * height"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Set the most significant zero bit of the integer.\n    If all bits are 1 up to the considered bit width, returns the original integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if value == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    bit_len = value.bit_length()\n    if value < 0:\n        max_bits = bit_len + 1\n    else:\n        max_bits = bit_len\n\n    for i in reversed(range(max_bits)):\n        if not (value >> i) & 1:\n            return value | (1 << i)\n\n    return value"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/388", "solution": "def highest_Power_of_2(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the greatest power of two that is less than or equal to n.\n    If n < 1, returns 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        return 0\n    return 1 << (n.bit_length() - 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/389", "solution": "def find_lucas(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n'th Lucas number using an iterative approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    a, b = 2, 1  # L(0) and L(1)\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    return b"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/390", "solution": "from typing import List, Any\n\ndef add_string(elements: List[Any], format_str: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Applies a format string to each element in a list, returning a new list of formatted strings.\n    Validates the format string for type and placeholder presence.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(format_str, str) or \"{0}\" not in format_str:\n        raise ValueError(\"The format_str must be a string and contain '{0}' placeholder.\")\n\n    return [format_str.format(element) for element in elements]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/392", "solution": "def get_max_sum(n, memo={}):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum possible by using the equation f(n) = max( (f(n/2) + f(n/3) + f(n/4) + f(n/5)), n).\n    Uses memoization to store and retrieve previously computed results.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n in memo:\n        return memo[n]\n\n    if n <= 1:\n        memo[n] = n\n        return n\n\n    # Recursively compute sums of sub-problems using integer division\n    sum_parts = (\n        get_max_sum(n // 2, memo) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 3, memo) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 4, memo) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 5, memo)\n    )\n\n    result = max(sum_parts, n)\n    memo[n] = result\n    return result"}
