{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "import math\n\ndef max_product(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of that array.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    # Initialize each dp[i] with nums[i] itself, as a single element is an increasing subsequence.\n    dp = list(nums)\n\n    # Initialize max_product_overall with the maximum single element found in the array.\n    # This handles cases where the optimal subsequence is a single element (e.g., all numbers decreasing).\n    max_product_overall = max(nums)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If nums[i] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at nums[j]\n            if nums[i] > nums[j]:\n                # Update dp[i] if multiplying nums[i] with the max product ending at j yields a larger product.\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * nums[i])\n        \n        # After considering all possible preceding subsequences for nums[i],\n        # update the overall maximum product found so far.\n        max_product_overall = max(max_product_overall, dp[i])\n    \n    return max_product_overall"}
