{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef find_remainder(elements: List[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the product of all elements in a list modulo n.\n    Applies modulo after each multiplication to prevent overflow.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not elements:\n        return 0 # As per FR2 and example behavior for empty product in modular arithmetic.\n    \n    # Initialize product to 1, as multiplying by 1 does not change the product.\n    # Note: Python's % operator handles negative numbers as required by FR3.\n    product = 1\n    for x in elements:\n        product = (product * x) % n\n        \n    return product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Set\n\ndef tuple_intersection(\n    list1: List[Tuple[int, int]],\n    list2: List[Tuple[int, int]]\n) -> Set[Tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the intersection of two lists of integer tuples, irrespective of their order.\n    Returns a set of unique common tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Normalize tuples to canonical order by sorting elements within each tuple.\n    # This ensures (3, 4) and (4, 3) are both represented as (3, 4).\n    norm1 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list1}\n    norm2 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list2}\n\n    # Compute the intersection of the two sets of normalized tuples.\n    return norm1 & norm2"}
